<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-10" top="0.5in" left="0.5in" right="0.5in" bottom="0.5in">
    <title>Lecture Worksheet - Mass Flow</title>

    <!-- Optional introduction -->
    <introduction>
        <p>
            This is the introduction to the activity.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <!-- Exercises start here. -->
     <exercise>
        <sidebyside widths="70% 30%">
            <p>
                    A chain of total mass <m>M</m> and length <m>L</m> is suspended vertically
                    with its lowest end touching a scale.
                    The chain is released and falls onto the scale.
                    What is the reading of the scale when a length of chain, <m>x</m>, has
                    fallen? (Neglect the size of individual links.)
            </p>
            <image source="images\activities\lec10\Falling_Chain.png">
                <shortdescription></shortdescription>
            </image>
        </sidebyside>
     </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A uniform rope of mass density <m>\lambda</m> per unit length is
                coiled on a smooth horizontal table.
                One end is pulled straight up with constant speed <m>v_{0}</m>,
                as shown in the figure
            </p>

            <image width="30%" source="images\activities\lec10\rope.png">
                <shortdescription>descr</shortdescription>
            </image>

            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Pick your system to be
                            <alert>the entire rope</alert>, which we'll say has
                            a total mass <m>M</m>.
                            Draw the FBD for the rope.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Discussion:</alert> What is the role of the
                            normal force in this problem?
                            To be more specific: once a length of rope <m>y</m>
                            has been pulled off the ground, what should the
                            magnitude of <m>\vec{N}</m> be?
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Write an expression for the system's momentum at a
                            time <m>t</m>.
                            Using Newton's 2nd law, find the magnitude of the
                            force <m>F</m> you need to exert to keep the rope
                            going at speed <m>v_{0}</m>.
                            Rewrite your expression in terms of <m>y</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Now let's make a different, time-dependent choice of
                            system.
                            Consider, at time t,
                            <alert>only the part of the rope that is off the ground</alert>.
                            Draw the FBD for this system.
                            What is <m>\dfrac{d\vec{P}}{dt}</m> for this system?
                            Note: when you take the derivative you
                            <alert>do not</alert> take into account that the
                            definition of the system changes with time - you
                            look at the system at a fixed time t and find ITS
                            time rate of change of <m>\vec{P}</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Extra: The tension at the bottom at the rope needs
                            to bring some amount of mass <m>dm</m> (how is
                            <m>dm</m> related to <m>dy</m>?) from rest to speed
                            <m>v_{0}</m> quickly.
                            See if you can use this idea to explain the
                            magnitude of the tension at the bottom of the rope
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>